Negative control for the gate collecting a session for an actor that can
actually sign in (`G6` · `D2` · `R6-15b`).

`useAuth`'s own matrix is the authority on what an auth journey may be collected
for: `AUTH_SCOPE_MATRIX` declares `SELF` and `GUEST` `never`, leaving CLIENT and
STAFF. The gate used to ask for the page's own scope, which on a bare url is
SELF — and SELF is a resolution, not an actor. The scope builder turns it into
the ACTIVE actor (`resolveSelfActor`), which for a visitor with no session is
GUEST; the machine's guest arm then mints a token and settles in the FINAL
`authenticated` state. Driven live on 2026-08-13 at a bare `/useClientEmails/`,
that is exactly what the operator's server drew: the overlay opened over the
page the guard had just refused and rendered "Authenticated · Successfully
authenticated as Self → Guest" with a single Log out button and no login form
(`canLogin` is `!isAuthenticated`, so the tab set was empty). Pressing that
button destroyed the journey, the overlay unmounted, and the url stayed on the
`--auth` child with nothing rendered — the page skeletonised at "showing 0 of 0"
with no login surface and no route back, recoverable only through the Sessions
pool or a hand-typed `auth/?as=client` url.

This mutant withdraws the resolution and hands back whatever the caller asked
for, so `SELF` survives to the journey again. Nothing else moves: the target is
still the page's own `--auth` child, still carries `mode` and `cancelUrl`, still
keeps `scopeSuffix`, and the pool's add-session arm still names its own actor —
which is why every claim in `scenario-auth-collect.spec.ts` stays green under it
and the loss needs a claim of its own.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the case asserting that the gate's re-target — and the overlay page's own
fallback for a target that names no actor — collect for an actor the auth matrix
declares, so a page reached with no `/as/<actor>` segment opens on a login rather
than on a signed-in guest. A staff url (`/as/user`) must still collect staff and
a guest url must still pass the guard untouched, so the claim cannot be met by
pinning one constant.

  Proven RED: owed by the prover's blind run (`vitest run scenario-auth-collect`).
  Recorded here rather than pre-filled — the seat that authors the mutant is not
  the seat that grades it.

Re-cut (`R7-1`): the GATE no longer names an actor when the url named none — it
offers the arrival a choice instead — so the case about a bare url must now
assert the chooser, and this control's claim narrows to what it always was: an
actor handed to `useAuth` is one the matrix declares. The add-session arm and the
overlay's own journey are where that now bites.

--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/app/funnels/labs.ts
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/app/funnels/labs.ts
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@
  * the client journey with no way to say otherwise.
  */
 export function authCollectActor(actor?: ScopeActorTypes): ScopeActorTypes {
-  return authNamedActor(actor) ?? ScopeActorTypes.CLIENT;
+  return (actor ?? ScopeActorTypes.SELF) as ScopeActorTypes;
 }
 
